            "content": "Implements **Task 4 — \"Define the target compatibility and trust contract\"** of\n[the public preview release plan](packages/storybook-addon-visual-tests/docs/2026-07-24-public-preview-release-plan.md)\n(Chunk 2, plan v4). Tasks 1 and 2 shipped in #244 and #245; Task 3 is a separate PR.\n\nTwo coupled outputs: a public-preview contract that separates **targeted** from\n**verified**, and fail-fast runtime validation for preset options.\n\n## Target support contract\n\n`README.md` gains a matrix whose every row is an explicit **release target**, paired\nwith the local evidence that exists today. Nothing is stated as verified support —\neach row stays a target until the packed-consumer CI matrix (Tasks 8/10) passes\nagainst the published tarball.\n\n| Dimension | Target | Local evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Node.js | `>=22.12` | 22.x (`.node-version` = `22.12.0`) |\n| Storybook | `^10.5.0` | 10.5.0 |\n| React | `^19.0.0` | 19.2.7 |\n| Framework | `@storybook/react-vite` | react-vite 10.5.0 (addon smoke fixture) |\n| Browser | bundled Playwright Chromium (`playwright` 1.55.1) | exercised |\n| OS | Linux x64; more only if baseline transfer passes | Linux x64 only |\n| Mode | local development server | exercised; static build reports unavailable |\n| Topology | direct loopback HTTP, same network namespace | exercised |\n\n**Cross-OS startup vs baseline portability** are documented as separate claims. The\nenvironment key omits the platform and baseline compatibility ignores the recorded\n`platform` field, so portability is an unproven design assumption; if the release\ngate's byte-for-byte transfer fails, preview support narrows to one OS.\n\n### Explicit non-goals\n\nHTTPS origins, reverse-proxy path prefixes, split-container/host capture, remote\napproval, non-Chromium browsers, viewport matrices, theme matrices, masking, and a\nCI runner.\n\nThe three network topologies are documented as **unsupported, not merely untested**:\nthe capture origin is built from the dev server's own port as\n`http://127.0.0.1:<port>` and is not configurable, so the implementation cannot\nreach them at all.\n\n### Trust boundary\n\nStated directly, with integrity separated from authorization:\n\n- Any party reaching the development manager channel may request runs and approvals;\n  the addon does not authenticate the requester.\n- Approval **writes repository files** (baseline PNG + metadata, beside the story).\n- Candidate SHA-256 hashes bind an approval to the exact captured bytes — **integrity,\n  not human identity**; they reject stale approvals, they do not authorize.\n- Git diff, commit review, and PR review authorize committed baseline changes.\n\nNo custom authentication was added, per the task.\n\n## Validation rules and defaults\n\nValidated in `experimental_serverChannel` **before** `presets.apply` and before the\nrunner is constructed, so bad config fails `storybook dev` at startup rather than\nmid-capture.\n\n| Option | Default when omitted | Accepted |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `storyRoots` | `[\".\"]` | non-empty array of non-empty strings |\n| `maxConcurrency` | `2` | integer `> 0`, no upper bound invented |\n\nRejected with actionable errors naming the option, the received value, and the\ndefault — e.g.\n`[storybook-addon-visual-tests] Invalid \"maxConcurrency\" preset option: expected an integer greater than 0, received NaN. Omit it to use the default 2.`\n\nBehavior change worth flagging: the old code used `options.storyRoots ?? [\".\"]` and\n`options.maxConcurrency ? {…} : {}`, so `null` and `0` silently fell back to defaults.\nThey are now hard errors. No in-repo consumer passes either\n(`apps/storybook/.storybook/main.ts` and the smoke fixture pass valid `storyRoots`\nand omit `maxConcurrency`).\n\n## Files intentionally excluded\n\n- `src/node/compare.ts`, `src/node/runner.ts`, `src/manager/PanelView.tsx`,\n  `test/compare.test.ts`, `test/runner.test.ts` — Task 3, owned by a parallel PR.\n- `ROADMAP.md` and the release plan — not modified; the new docs were checked against\n  them for contradictions.\n- `package.json` publish metadata (name, version, description, keywords, repository,\n  license, `publishConfig`, `private`) and peer-range finalization — assigned to Tasks\n  9 and 10, which need Task 8/10 evidence. The only manifest change is\n  `engines.node: \">=22.12\"`, which is the machine-readable form of the matrix's Node\n  row and is backed by `.node-version` and the root manifest; without it the manifest\n  and the documented Node target disagree.\n- Options are documented only in `docs/configuration.md`, not duplicated into\n  `README.md`, to avoid two copies of the defaults drifting apart.\n\n## TDD evidence\n\nRed first, on `test/server.test.ts`:\n\n```\npnpm --filter @workspace/storybook-addon-visual-tests test server\n→ Tests  21 failed | 6 passed (27)\n```\n\nAll 21 new assertions failed against the unvalidated preset (the one pre-existing\npass was \"accepts valid explicit values\", which the old code already satisfied).\nAfter the minimal implementation:\n\n```\n→ Tests  33 passed (33)\n```\n\nCoverage: defaults with both options omitted; valid explicit values; and rejection of\n— `storyRoots`: string, object, `null`, number, empty array, empty-string member,\nwhitespace-only member, mixed array, `null` member, `undefined` member, all-holes\nsparse array, leading hole; `maxConcurrency`: string, `0`, negative, fractional,\n`NaN`, `Infinity`, `-Infinity`, `null`, boolean, object, boxed `new Number(2)`,\nbigint. Each rejection also asserts the runner was never constructed **and**\n`presets.apply` was never called, which is what proves \"before any work starts\".\n\n## Primary sources\n\nCompatibility and API claims were checked against local primary sources, not peer\nranges or memory:\n\n- **Storybook preset options merging** — `applyPresets` in the installed\n  `storybook@10.5.0` (`dist/_node-chunks/chunk-VFV5MDK3.js:13582`) builds\n  `combinedOptions = {...storybookOptions, ...args, ...options}`, confirming addon\n  options land unvalidated in the second argument.\n- **Throw fails startup** — `dist/core-server/index.js:11984` awaits\n  `presets.apply(\"experimental_serverChannel\", channel)` during dev bootstrap, before\n  `storybookDevServer`.\n- **Versions** — `.node-version`, root `package.json`, `pnpm-workspace.yaml` catalog,\n  `pnpm-lock.yaml` (`storybook@10.5.0`, `react@19.2.7`, `@storybook/react-vite@10.5.0`,\n  `playwright@1.55.1`), and `test/fixtures/project/.storybook/main.ts` (react-vite).\n- **OS evidence** — `.github/workflows/ci.yml` is `ubuntu-latest` only.\n- **Comparator/compatibility/failure text** — `src/node/compare.ts`\n  (`COMPARATOR_POLICY`, `compatibleMetadata`, `incompatibilityReason`) and\n  `src/node/runner.ts` (`Chromium could not start: …`).\n\ncontext7 was consulted for the Storybook preset API; `experimental_serverChannel` is\nundocumented there, so the installed source was used instead and the API is treated as\nunstable rather than a basis for broad compatibility claims.\n\n## Self-review and adversarial review\n\nSelf-review of the full diff found two real validation holes, both fixed with tests\nbefore requesting review:\n\n1. **Sparse arrays bypassed validation entirely.** The first implementation used\n   `value.map(...)`, and `.map` skips array holes — `storyRoots: new Array(3)` passed\n   and produced a sparse array that would have failed later inside path resolution.\n   Replaced with an indexed loop. Verified empirically: the `.map` version accepts\n   `new Array(3)`, the indexed version rejects index 0.\n2. **The value formatter was misleading.** It rendered objects as \"a object\" and a\n   bigint `2n` as a bare `2`. It now reports \"an object\"/\"a function\" and\n   `2 (bigint)`, and never reads the value's own properties, so a circular or\n   null-prototype object still yields the intended error instead of a `TypeError`.\n\nTwo independent adversarial reviewer